arm64: dts: khadas-edge: the hardware RNG in crypto1

Khadas Edge1, Android 14 on mainline 6.12. Applied to kernel/mainline by
build/build-kernel.sh before every build (device/khadas/edge/kernel/patches/).

The node for patch 0009's driver, where Rockchip's own DT has it: rng@ff8b8000,
the second crypto engine, with its crypto clock at 150 MHz and the slave AHB
clock at 100 MHz as there. The engine itself has no driver in this build
(CONFIG_CRYPTO_DEV_ROCKCHIP is not set), and the RNG takes its registers, so
crypto1 is disabled; crypto0 stays as it is.

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3399-khadas-edge.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3399-khadas-edge.dtsi
index 8ea31a0dd..c6ad36e18 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3399-khadas-edge.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3399-khadas-edge.dtsi
@@ -944,3 +944,26 @@ &vopl {
 &vopl_mmu {
 	status = "disabled";
 };
+
+/*
+ * The hardware RNG: the TRNG in the second crypto block, as Rockchip's own DT
+ * has it (rng@ff8b8000). Nothing in this build drives the crypto blocks
+ * themselves (CRYPTO_DEV_ROCKCHIP is off), and the RNG takes crypto1's
+ * registers, so crypto1 goes. Without it there is no /dev/hw_random, and
+ * Android's prng_seeder fails at every boot.
+ */
+&crypto1 {
+	status = "disabled";
+};
+
+/ {
+	rng@ff8b8000 {
+		compatible = "rockchip,rk3399-crypto-rng";
+		reg = <0x0 0xff8b8000 0x0 0x1000>;
+		clocks = <&cru SCLK_CRYPTO1>, <&cru HCLK_S_CRYPTO1>;
+		clock-names = "core", "ahb";
+		assigned-clocks = <&cru SCLK_CRYPTO1>, <&cru HCLK_S_CRYPTO1>;
+		assigned-clock-rates = <150000000>, <100000000>;
+		resets = <&cru SRST_CRYPTO1>;
+	};
+};
